  "_id": "6a7f5797757ee51246381c72",
  "lab": "6a7f5796eda1a5feaff0b458",
  "name": "HCV Rapid",
  "code": "HCV RAPID",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table><thead><tr><th><div>HCV Rapid Test Result</div></th><th><div>Interpretation</div></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><div>Reactive</div></td><td><div>A reactive HCV rapid test result indicates the presence of antibodies against the Hepatitis C virus in the blood. This suggests either a current or past HCV infection. Further confirmation with a supplemental test, such as an HCV RNA test, is required to determine if the infection is active (chronic) or has been cleared by the immune system (resolved). If the HCV RNA test is positive, it indicates an active HCV infection, and appropriate medical evaluation and management are necessary. If the HCV RNA test is negative, it suggests a resolved infection, meaning that the individual has cleared the virus and is no longer actively infected.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Non-Reactive</div></td><td><div>A non-reactive HCV rapid test result means that no antibodies against the Hepatitis C virus were detected in the blood sample. This result is typically seen in individuals who have not been exposed to HCV or those who have not been infected with HCV in the past. However, it's important to note that during the window period (the time between HCV infection and the development of detectable antibodies), the rapid test may show a non-reactive result even if an active HCV infection is present. In such cases, follow-up testing may be necessary after a few weeks to detect the antibodies.</div></td></tr></tbody></table>",
